**Clarify:** capacity, behaviour on ties (evict the least recently used among the lowest frequency), whether `put` on an existing key counts as a use (yes), and the required time complexity.

**O(1) design.**

- `Map<Integer, Node> keyMap`: key to its entry (value and frequency).
- `Map<Integer, LinkedHashSet<Integer>> freqMap`: frequency to the keys with that frequency, in insertion order (so the first is the least recently used).
- `int minFreq`: the lowest frequency currently present.

**Operations.**

- `get(k)`: if absent return -1. Otherwise bump the frequency: remove `k` from `freqMap[f]`; if that set became empty and `f == minFreq`, increment `minFreq`; add `k` to `freqMap[f+1]`; return the value.
- `put(k, v)`: if capacity is 0 return. If `k` exists, update the value and bump the frequency. Otherwise, if full, evict the first key of `freqMap[minFreq]` (and delete it from `keyMap`). Insert the new key with frequency 1 and set `minFreq = 1`.

```java
class LFUCache {
    private final int cap; private int minFreq;
    private final Map<Integer, int[]> kv = new HashMap<>();            // key -> {value, freq}
    private final Map<Integer, LinkedHashSet<Integer>> freq = new HashMap<>();
    LFUCache(int c) { cap = c; }

    private void touch(int k) {
        int[] e = kv.get(k); int f = e[1];
        LinkedHashSet<Integer> s = freq.get(f); s.remove(k);
        if (s.isEmpty()) { freq.remove(f); if (minFreq == f) minFreq++; }
        e[1] = f + 1;
        freq.computeIfAbsent(f + 1, x -> new LinkedHashSet<>()).add(k);
    }
    int get(int k) { if (!kv.containsKey(k)) return -1; touch(k); return kv.get(k)[0]; }
    void put(int k, int v) {
        if (cap == 0) return;
        if (kv.containsKey(k)) { kv.get(k)[0] = v; touch(k); return; }
        if (kv.size() == cap) {
            LinkedHashSet<Integer> s = freq.get(minFreq);
            int old = s.iterator().next(); s.remove(old);
            if (s.isEmpty()) freq.remove(minFreq);
            kv.remove(old);
        }
        kv.put(k, new int[]{v, 1}); minFreq = 1;
        freq.computeIfAbsent(1, x -> new LinkedHashSet<>()).add(k);
    }
}
```

**Alternatives.** A min-heap keyed by (frequency, last-used time) is simpler but O(log n). A custom doubly linked list per frequency avoids `LinkedHashSet` overhead.

**Edge cases:** capacity 0, updating an existing key, and not resetting `minFreq` when a bump empties a lower bucket.
